    "abstract": "This research investigates the role of Product Language — how products communicate meaning through their material, formal, and aesthetic qualities — in shaping and reinforcing stereotypical and stigmatising social representations within industrial product design. Starting from the premise that design is not neutral, the study examines how visual and formal conventions stabilise into design archetypes that may perpetuate discriminatory norms related to gender, race, age, or ability. Positioned at the intersection of Product Language studies and critical design approaches concerned with social justice and inclusivity, the research aims to develop a conceptual framework and practical orientation for designers to navigate Product Language choices more critically, sensitively, and inclusively."
